<section>
    <h2>1. Introduction</h2>

```
<section>
    <h3>1.1 Purpose</h3>
    <p>
        This document describes the main requirements, architecture and user interaction
        model for SOr – Soft Drink Web Organizer, a web application designed for managing
        preferences regarding non-alcoholic drinks.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h3>1.2 Document Conventions</h3>
    <p>
        The document follows the structure of the IEEE Software Requirements Specification
        template and is written in Scholarly HTML format.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h3>1.3 Intended Audience and Reading Suggestions</h3>
    <p>
        The document is intended for project evaluators, developers and users interested in
        understanding the system requirements, user roles and application architecture.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h3>1.4 Product Scope</h3>
    <p>
        SOr is a web application for managing user preferences related to non-alcoholic drinks,
        such as coffee, tea, milk-based drinks, juices and smoothies. The application allows users
        to discover drinks, manage personal preferences, create wishlist and tried drink lists,
        view statistics and export data.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h3>1.5 References</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>IEEE System Requirements Specification Template</li>
        <li>Scholarly HTML</li>
        <li>PostgreSQL Documentation</li>
        <li>PHP Documentation</li>
        <li>HTML, CSS and JavaScript standards</li>
    </ul>
</section>
```

</section>

<section>
    <h2>2. Overall Description</h2>

```
<section>
    <h3>2.1 Product Perspective</h3>
    <p>
        The application is a client-server web system. The frontend is implemented using HTML,
        CSS and JavaScript, while the backend is implemented in PHP. Data is stored in a
        PostgreSQL relational database. The application is containerized using Docker.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h3>2.2 Product Functions</h3>
    <p>
        The main functionalities of the system include drink discovery, drink management,
        preference management, user administration, statistics generation and export in CSV,
        SVG and RSS formats.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h3>2.3 User Classes and Characteristics</h3>

    <h4>User</h4>
    <p>
        Regular users can search and view drinks, inspect drink details, add drinks to wishlist,
        mark drinks as tried, add ratings and comments, favorite providers, manage profile
        information, view a personal dashboard and consult global statistics.
    </p>

    <h4>Provider</h4>
    <p>
        Providers represent local businesses such as cafes, restaurants, bars or markets. They
        can view drinks, manage their own profile, access a provider dashboard, create drinks,
        edit drinks, delete drinks, manage ingredients and view received reviews.
    </p>

    <h4>Administrator</h4>
    <p>
        Administrators have full access to the system. They can manage users, providers, drinks,
        categories and ingredients. They can edit or delete entities, access the administrative
        dashboard and consult global statistics.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h3>2.4 Operating Environment</h3>
    <p>
        The application runs in a Docker environment and uses Nginx, PHP and PostgreSQL.
        Users access the system through a modern web browser.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h3>2.5 Design and Implementation Constraints</h3>
    <p>
        The project uses free and open-source technologies. The frontend must be responsive and
        the backend follows a layered architecture based on controllers, services and repositories.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h3>2.6 User Documentation</h3>
    <p>
        Users interact with the application through role-based pages and dashboards. The interface
        provides forms, tables, cards and charts for managing and visualizing data.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h3>2.7 Assumptions and Dependencies</h3>
    <p>
        The application assumes that the database is available, Docker containers are running and
        the frontend can communicate with the backend API.
    </p>
</section>
```

</section>

<section>
    <h2>3. External Interface Requirements</h2>

```
<section>
    <h3>3.1 User Interfaces</h3>
    <p>
        The interface is responsive and role-based. Users, providers and administrators have
        access to different dashboards and actions. The design uses cards, tables, navigation
        menus and charts to make information easy to understand.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h3>3.2 Hardware Interfaces</h3>
    <p>
        No special hardware interfaces are required. The system can be accessed from desktop or
        mobile devices through a web browser.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h3>3.3 Software Interfaces</h3>
    <p>
        The system communicates with a PostgreSQL database through the PHP backend. The frontend
        communicates with the backend using HTTP requests to REST-like API endpoints.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h3>3.4 Communications Interfaces</h3>
    <p>
        Communication between frontend and backend is performed through HTTP. Authentication is
        handled using tokens. The RSS feed is exposed as an XML endpoint.
    </p>
</section>
```

</section>

<section>
    <h2>4. System Features</h2>

```
<section>
    <h3>4.1 Drink Discovery and Search</h3>
    <p>
        Users can search for drinks and view drink details, including name, category, provider,
        price, ingredients, image and reviews.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h3>4.2 Preference Management</h3>
    <p>
        Users can add drinks to wishlist, mark drinks as tried, add ratings and comments, select
        favorite categories, favorite ingredients, avoided ingredients, restrictions and favorite
        providers.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h3>4.3 Provider Drink Management</h3>
    <p>
        Providers can create, edit and delete their own drinks. They can also manage drink
        ingredients and view reviews received from users.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h3>4.4 Administration</h3>
    <p>
        Administrators can manage users, providers, drinks, categories and ingredients. They have
        access to an administrative dashboard and global statistics.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h3>4.5 Statistics and Exports</h3>
    <p>
        The system generates statistics about top drinks, top categories, top providers, top
        ingredients, avoided ingredients and restrictions. These statistics can be exported as
        CSV and SVG. The ranking of the most popular drinks is also available as an RSS feed.
    </p>
</section>
```

</section>

<section>
    <h2>5. Other Nonfunctional Requirements</h2>

```
<section>
    <h3>5.1 Performance Requirements</h3>
    <p>
        The application should respond in a reasonable time for common operations such as login,
        browsing drinks, loading dashboards and generating statistics.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h3>5.2 Safety Requirements</h3>
    <p>
        The system should avoid accidental data loss by validating delete and update operations
        and restricting actions based on user roles.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h3>5.3 Security Requirements</h3>
    <p>
        Authentication is required for protected operations. Role-based access control is used
        to separate user, provider and administrator permissions.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h3>5.4 Software Quality Attributes</h3>
    <p>
        The application is structured using a layered architecture. Controllers handle requests,
        services contain application logic, repositories interact with the database, and export
        services generate CSV, SVG and RSS outputs.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h3>5.5 Business Rules</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Users can manage only their own preferences.</li>
        <li>Providers can manage their own drinks.</li>
        <li>Administrators can manage all entities.</li>
        <li>Statistics are generated from stored user preferences and drink interactions.</li>
    </ul>
</section>
```

</section>

<section>
    <h2>6. Other Requirements</h2>
    <p>
        The project must include architecture diagrams, database diagrams, a responsive web interface,
        a working implementation and a short demo video.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Appendix A: Glossary</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Wishlist</strong> – list of drinks the user wants to try.</li>
        <li><strong>Tried drinks</strong> – drinks already consumed and rated by the user.</li>
        <li><strong>Provider</strong> – business that offers drinks.</li>
        <li><strong>RSS</strong> – XML feed containing the ranking of popular drinks.</li>
        <li><strong>CSV</strong> – textual format used for exporting tabular data.</li>
        <li><strong>SVG</strong> – vector graphic format used for exporting charts.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Appendix B: Analysis Models</h2>
    <p>
        The documentation includes the following diagrams:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>C4 Context Diagram</li>
